diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 9b5ce1a..86fb158 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -384,6 +384,15 @@ var primitiveValues = qs.parse('a=15&b=true&c=null');
 assert.deepEqual(primitiveValues, { a: '15', b: 'true', c: 'null' });
 ```
 
+The `parseNumbers` option can be used to convert string values to numbers when the string is in canonical form. When `parseNumbers: true`, a value is replaced with its numeric equivalent only if `String(Number(value)) === value` (and the number is finite). This conversion is applied after decoding and works with arrays, nested objects, and the `comma` option. Keys are never converted. `null` values (from `strictNullHandling`) and values from an object (non-string) input are left as they are. For example:
+
+```javascript
+var numericValues = qs.parse('a=1&b=-2.5&c=01&d=1e3&e=abc', { parseNumbers: true });
+assert.deepStrictEqual(numericValues, { a: 1, b: -2.5, c: '01', d: '1e3', e: 'abc' });
+```
+
+In this example, `a`, `b` are converted because their string forms are canonical (1, -2.5), but `c=01`, `d=1e3` (non-canonical exponential form), and `e=abc` (non-numeric) remain as strings.
+
 If you wish to auto-convert values which look like numbers, booleans, and other values into their primitive counterparts, you can use the [query-types Express JS middleware](https://github.com/xpepermint/query-types) which will auto-convert all request query parameters.
 
